      │
      ▼
Data Breach

Why Architecture Allowed This?
The system trusted user-controlled filenames to construct filesystem paths.
Developers assumed users would only request files inside /app/reports.

Mitigation:
Use indirect file references:
/download?id=42

Internal mapping:
42 → /app/reports/report.pdf

Also verify canonical paths.

2️⃣ Path Traversal → Cloud Credential Theft → Cloud Account Compromise
This is extremely common in modern cloud workloads.
Architecture:
Internet User
     │
     ▼
Web API
     │
     ▼
File Preview Service
GET /preview?file=image.jpg
     │
     ▼
Application Container
     │
     ▼
Container Filesystem
     │
     ▼
Cloud Credentials File

Containers often contain credentials like:
~/.aws/credentials

Step-by-Step Attack:
Normal request:
GET /preview?file=image.jpg
Server reads:
/images/image.jpg

Attacker attempts traversal:
GET /preview?file=../../../../root/.aws/credentials
OS resolves path:
/images/../../../../root/.aws/credentials

Final file accessed:
/root/.aws/credentials

This file contains:
AWS_ACCESS_KEY
AWS_SECRET_KEY

The attacker now has cloud API credentials.

Full Attack Chain:
Path Traversal
      │
      ▼
Cloud Credential File Read
      │
      ▼
AWS API Access
      │
      ▼
Create Compute / Access Storage
      │
      ▼
Cloud Account Compromise

Why This Happens?
Developers assume containers isolate secrets.
But containers still expose local files unless access is restricted.

Mitigation:
Store secrets in:
environment variables
secret managers
short-lived tokens
Also restrict filesystem access.

3️⃣ Path Traversal → Kubernetes Token Theft → Cluster Takeover
This is a real modern cloud attack pattern.
Architecture:
Internet
   │
   ▼
Microservice API
   │
   ▼
File Reader Endpoint
   │
   ▼
Container Runtime
   │
   ▼
Kubernetes Service Account

Token stored at:
/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token

Attack:
Attacker sends:
GET /file?name=../../../../var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token
Application reads the file.
Response contains:
Kubernetes API Token
Attacker now calls the Kubernetes API:
kubectl --token=<stolen token>

Possible actions:
create pods
read secrets
deploy containers

Full Attack Chain:
Path Traversal
      │
      ▼
Service Account Token Theft
      │
      ▼
Kubernetes API Access
      │
      ▼
Deploy Malicious Pod
      │
      ▼
Cluster Compromise

Why Architecture Failed?
Kubernetes automatically mounts service tokens in pods.
Applications did not restrict filesystem reads.

Mitigation:
Disable auto-mount tokens when not required:
automountServiceAccountToken: false
Also validate file paths strictly.

